.\" Manual page for swinv(8) - local software inventory collector.
.\" Rendered with: man ./packaging/swinv.8
.Dd August 19, 2026
.Dt SWINV 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm swinv
.Nd collect a local software inventory
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl -root Ar path
.Op Fl -out Ar dir
.Op Fl -name Ar template
.Op Fl -output-mode Ar mode
.Op Fl -format Ar list
.Op Fl -stdout
.Op Fl -latest-symlink Ns = Ns Ar bool
.Op Fl -exclude Ar glob
.Op Fl -no-auto-exclude-mounts
.Op Fl -no-snap
.Op Fl -no-flatpak
.Op Fl -include-home
.Op Fl -offline
.Op Fl -perm Ar octal
.Op Fl -skip-nested-rootfs
.Op Fl -max-memory Ar size
.Op Fl -hash
.Op Fl -since Ar path
.Op Fl -delta-only
.Op Fl -ndjson-include Ar list
.Op Fl -heartbeat
.Op Fl -force-full
.Op Fl -full-interval Ar duration
.Op Fl -catalogers Ar expr
.Op Fl -no-file-ownership
.Op Fl -no-services
.Op Fl -no-containers
.Op Fl -no-service-command
.Op Fl -usn-probe
.Op Fl -volumes Ar spec
.Op Fl -debug-stacks-after Ar duration
.Op Fl -fast
.Op Fl -parallelism Ar n
.Op Fl -timeout Ar duration
.Op Fl -require-host-id
.Op Fl -quiet
.Op Fl -verbose
.Nm
.Fl -version
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
scans the machine it runs on, enumerates every piece of installed software it
can find - operating-system packages, language-ecosystem packages, and loose
binaries that no package manager ever installed - and writes the result to
local files.
.Pp
There is no server, no agent, no database, and no network transmission.
Files land on disk and something else
.Pq Xr rsync 1 , Ansible, a log shipper, a human
is responsible for collecting them later.
.Pp
.Nm
is a single static binary. It needs no runtime, no interpreter, and no
configuration file. Running as a non-root user is fully supported: the scan
simply misses root-only paths and DMI serial numbers, and records a warning in
the report.
.Pp
All human-readable output goes to standard error. Standard output carries data
only, and only when
.Fl -stdout
is given.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl -root Ar path
Filesystem root to scan.
Default:
.Pa / .
Point it at a mounted image or a fixture tree to scan something other than the
running system. Component locations are reported as absolute system paths with
this prefix stripped.
.It Fl -out Ar dir
Output directory. Created with mode 0755 if missing; files are written 0644.
Default:
.Pa /var/lib/swinv .
.It Fl -name Ar template
Basename for the output files, without an extension. The following
placeholders are substituted:
.Bl -tag -width "{machine_id}" -compact -offset indent
.It Sy {hostname}
the short hostname
.It Sy {machine_id}
contents of
.Pa /etc/machine-id
.It Sy {date}
UTC date,
.Ql 20060102
.It Sy {datetime}
UTC timestamp,
.Ql 20060102T150405.000Z
.El
.Pp
When this flag is not given, the default template comes from
.Fl -output-mode .
An explicit
.Fl -name
always wins.
.It Fl -output-mode Ar mode
Selects the default
.Fl -name
template, and with it the file-rotation behaviour.
Default:
.Cm timestamped .
.Bl -tag -width "timestamped" -offset indent
.It Cm timestamped
Template
.Ql {hostname}-{datetime} .
A new file for every run, kept until something prunes them.
This is the default because a report is evidence of what was installed at a
moment, and a second run on the same day should not silently replace the first.
.It Cm dated
Template
.Ql {hostname}-{date} .
One file per day; re-running on the same day replaces that day's file.
.It Cm overwrite
Template
.Ql {hostname} .
One fixed file, replaced on every run.
.El
.Pp
All three modes write atomically, so
.Cm overwrite
never leaves a partially written file behind.
.It Fl -format Ar list
Comma-separated list of output formats.
Default:
.Ql json,csv .
Recognised values:
.Bl -tag -width "cyclonedx-json" -compact -offset indent
.It Cm json
the full report, indented, extension
.Pa .json
.It Cm csv
one row per component, extension
.Pa .csv
.It Cm ndjson
one JSON object per component per line, extension
.Pa .ndjson
.It Cm cyclonedx-json
CycloneDX 1.6, extension
.Pa .cdx.json
.El
.It Fl -stdout
Write to standard output instead of to files. Requires exactly one
.Fl -format .
.It Fl -latest-symlink
Also maintain
.Pa {hostname}-latest.<ext>
symlinks in the output directory, pointing at the file just written. The
symlink is replaced atomically.
Default: enabled; disable with
.Fl -latest-symlink Ns = Ns Cm false .
Most useful together with
.Fl -output-mode Cm timestamped .
.It Fl -exclude Ar glob
Exclude a path from the scan. Repeatable, and appended to the built-in
defaults. Patterns must begin with
.Ql ./ ,
.Ql */ ,
or
.Ql **/ ;
anything else is a usage error.
.It Fl -no-auto-exclude-mounts
Do not automatically exclude mount points whose filesystem is not local
.Pq nfs, cifs, sshfs, autofs, overlay, squashfs, and friends .
Reading
.Pa /proc/self/mountinfo
and skipping those mounts is the single largest factor in whether a scan takes
seconds or hours; turn this off only deliberately.
.It Fl -no-snap
Exclude
.Pa /snap .
.It Fl -no-flatpak
Exclude
.Pa /var/lib/flatpak .
.Pp
Both trees hold entire vendored runtimes. They are genuinely installed
software and are included by default, at the cost of scan time and component
count.
.Pp
Note that snaps are squashfs loop mounts and squashfs is in the non-local
filesystem skip list;
.Nm
carves snap mount points out of that rule so the two behaviours do not
contradict each other.
.It Fl -include-home
Also scan the user home trees
.Pa /home
and
.Pa /root .
They are skipped by default: they are typically larger than the rest of the
filesystem combined, dominated by
.Pa node_modules
and virtualenv trees, and they are per-user, high-churn and privacy-sensitive.
The skipped paths are always recorded in
.Va scan.excluded
and announced in
.Va scan.warnings ,
so a consumer can tell what was left out.
.It Fl -offline
Skip the reverse-DNS lookup used to fill
.Va host.fqdn .
That lookup is the only network activity
.Nm
performs; it carries no inventory data, but it does tell the configured
resolver that this host looked itself up.
With this flag the run is completely network-silent, at the cost of one field.
It is skipped automatically whenever
.Fl -root
is not
.Pa / .
.It Fl -max-memory Ar size
Set a soft memory limit, for example
.Cm 512MiB
or
.Cm 2GiB .
Go's garbage collector then works proportionally harder as the limit is
approached, trading CPU time for resident memory.
This is a soft limit: if the genuinely live data exceeds it the process still
allocates rather than failing, because a truncated inventory would be worse
than a larger one. It reduces peak memory; it does not guarantee a ceiling.
.It Fl -skip-nested-rootfs
Drop components whose package-database evidence comes from a second root
filesystem stored inside the one being scanned - an extracted image, a
container rootfs, a chroot, or a test fixture.
Such packages are otherwise reported as installed and carry this host's
distribution label.
Off by default, because scanning such a tree is sometimes exactly the intent;
.Nm
warns about them either way.
.It Fl -perm Ar octal
Permission bits for the report files, default
.Cm 0644 .
The output directory receives the same bits plus execute wherever read is
granted, so
.Cm 0644
yields a
.Cm 0755
directory,
.Cm 0640
yields
.Cm 0750 ,
and
.Cm 0600
yields
.Cm 0700 .
The permissive default lets a collector running as another user read the
reports; tighten it if any local user reading the inventory is a concern.
Setuid, setgid and the sticky bit are refused rather than silently dropped.
.It Fl -hash
Record a SHA-256 digest of each component's primary on-disk file in the
.Va sha256
field and CSV column.
Off by default: it reads every such file in full.
Files backing more than one component (package databases such as
.Pa /var/lib/dpkg/status )
are deliberately not hashed, because a digest shared by every package on the
machine identifies none of them and would make all of them appear to change
whenever any one package changed.
.It Fl -since Ar path
Compare this scan against a previous
.Nm
JSON report and add a
.Va delta
block listing added, removed and version-changed components.
Components are matched on name and type, not version, so an upgrade is
reported as one change rather than a removal plus an addition.
The full inventory is still written alongside the delta.
.It Fl -delta-only
With
.Fl -since ,
emit only the changed components instead of the full inventory. Each is tagged
.Cm added , Cm removed No or Cm changed
in the
.Va change
field.
Requires
.Fl -since .
.It Fl -ndjson-include Ar list
Carry more than components in the NDJSON stream:
.Ql exposure ,
.Ql containers ,
or
.Ql all .
.Pp
NDJSON is one component per line, so
.Ql exposure
and
.Ql containers
- which the JSON document and the CSV sidecars both carry - are missing
from the one output shape built for streaming. Each extra record carries a
.Ql record_type
an older consumer can skip; a line without one is a component. Off by default
for that reason.
.Pp
.Ql exposure
is one record per port per package behind it, so a finding joins on the package
without unpacking an array. A port with nothing attributed still produces a
record: that is a gap in what can be seen, not a port that is safe.
.Ql containers
includes stopped ones, whose vulnerabilities are latent rather than absent.
.It Fl -heartbeat
Emit a one-line digest of the inventory into the NDJSON stream on every scan,
and the component records only when that digest changes.
.Pp
Every scan otherwise restates the whole inventory, which is correct - a
package that disappears is genuinely gone rather than merely unmentioned -
and expensive: five thousand hosts averaging fourteen thousand components,
scanned hourly, is over a billion records a day, nearly all identical to the
day before.
.Pp
Only NDJSON is affected. JSON, CSV and CycloneDX carry the full inventory every
time, because a CSV with no rows would be a false statement about the machine.
When anything does change the whole list is sent again, never a delta: a delta
cannot express a removal.
.Pp
This is the only thing
.Nm
remembers between runs, in
.Pa .swinv-heartbeat.json
under
.Fl -out .
Default: off.
.It Fl -force-full
With
.Fl -heartbeat ,
send the component records even when the inventory is unchanged.
.It Fl -full-interval Ar duration
With
.Fl -heartbeat ,
send the full list at least this often however unchanged it looks, so that a
digest collision or a hand-edited state file cannot hide a change
indefinitely. Zero never forces one.
Default: 24h.
.It Fl -catalogers Ar expr
Cataloger selection expression, in
.Ql +name,-tag
syntax. Empty means the default selection, which is the
.Ql installed
and
.Ql directory
tags - already correct for a live filesystem, and already covering loose
binaries. Example:
.Ql --catalogers os
for operating-system packages only.
.It Fl -no-file-ownership
Skip package-to-file ownership resolution. Faster, but it reintroduces
duplicate components: the binary classifier will report files that an OS
package already claims.
Default: ownership resolution is on.
.It Fl -no-services
Do not report what is listening on the network.
Linux only; the collector reads
.Pa /proc
and does nothing elsewhere.
By default the report carries a
.Ql services
array naming each listening socket, the process behind it, its systemd unit or
container, and which installed software owns its executable.
Collecting it costs milliseconds and requires reading
.Pa /proc/<pid>/fd
for processes
.Nm
does not own, which needs root; unprivileged, the ports are still reported and
the processes behind them mostly are not.
Default: services are collected.
.It Fl -no-containers
Do not identify containers or what they run.
On Linux
.Nm
reads each container's own package database through
.Pa /proc/<pid>/root
to name the software behind a published port, which is the only identity a
vulnerability matcher can use for a containerised service - an image
reference is not one.
.Pp
On Windows a container is a Linux process inside a virtual machine that no
Windows API reaches, so
.Nm
asks the local Docker engine over its named pipe instead. That names the
container, the image and the published port mappings, but not the packages
inside; the report records
.Ql container-packages-not-readable
in its blind spots. This is the only daemon
.Nm
talks to, and it is local IPC, not network activity.
.Pp
Disabling it keeps the host services and the exposure list and loses the
container identities.
Default: containers are inspected.
.It Fl -no-service-command
Omit each service's command line from the report, keeping everything else.
A command line is often the only identification available for an interpreted
daemon, where the executable names the runtime and the application is named in
the arguments. It is also where secrets end up - a password on a daemon's
ExecStart, a connection string with credentials in it - and an inventory file
is usually copied somewhere with a different audience.
Default: command lines are recorded.
.It Fl -usn-probe
Windows only, experimental.
Enumerate the NTFS Master File Table and report what it contains, without
scanning and without opening any file.
Requires an elevated process and an NTFS volume.
Exits 2 on any other platform.
.It Fl -volumes Ar spec
Windows only.
Comma-separated volumes to enumerate, such as
.Ql D:
or
.Ql D:,E: .
Replaces the default of
.Ql C:
rather than adding to it: naming volumes selects exactly those.
Currently applies only to
.Fl -usn-probe .
.It Fl -debug-stacks-after Ar duration
If the scan is still running after
.Ar duration ,
write every goroutine stack to a file in the output directory and continue.
For diagnosing a scan that appears to have hung, where
.Dv SIGQUIT
or Ctrl+Break is not reachable -- from a timer unit, a scheduled task, or a
keyboard with no Break key.
Default:
.Ql 0 ,
never.
.It Fl -fast
Scan at normal scheduling priority and full parallelism.
.Pp
By default
.Nm
treats a scan as background maintenance and steps out of the way of everything
else on the machine: it lowers CPU priority to
.Ql nice 10 ,
requests the idle I/O scheduling class, and uses a quarter of the available
CPUs as cataloger workers. This makes scans roughly a third slower and is the
right trade for an unattended run on a machine doing real work.
.Pp
The idle I/O class is only honoured by the BFQ scheduler; under
.Ql mq-deadline
or
.Ql none
it has no effect and only the nice value applies.
Default: background priority.
.It Fl -parallelism Ar n
Number of concurrent catalogers.
.Ql 0
selects automatically: a quarter of the CPUs, or one per CPU with
.Fl -fast .
An explicit value overrides both, and may exceed the CPU count.
Default:
.Ql 0 .
.It Fl -timeout Ar duration
Whole-run deadline, as a Go duration string.
If the scan cannot be cancelled cooperatively -- filesystem indexing takes no
context and cannot be interrupted -- a watchdog terminates the process ten
seconds past the deadline.
.Pp
Given as
.Pq Ql 90s , Ql 30m , Ql 2h .
Default:
.Ql 30m .
Exceeding it exits 4.
.It Fl -require-host-id
Fail if
.Pa /etc/machine-id
is unreadable. Off by default, since every host fact is optional.
.It Fl -quiet
Suppress status output on standard error.
.It Fl -verbose
Print per-stage timings - source construction, cataloging, writing - to
standard error, so a performance regression is attributable.
.It Fl -transmit Ar url
Also POST this scan to a Riskability server, for example
.Ql https://riskability.example/api/v1 .
The report files under
.Fl -out
are written exactly as they would be without it: transmission adds a
destination, it does not replace one.
The NDJSON is spooled under
.Pa <out>/.swinv-spool
first, batched by
.Fl -transmit-batch-lines
and
.Fl -transmit-batch-bytes
(whichever trips first), gzipped, and removed only once the server has accepted
and reconciled it - so a collector that dies mid-upload resumes rather than
rescanning.
Refused with
.Fl -offline , -stdout
and
.Fl -delta-only .
.It Fl -transmit-token-file Ar path
Read the bearer token from this file, or set
.Ev SWINV_TRANSMIT_TOKEN .
There is deliberately no flag for the token itself: a command line is readable
by every user on the machine.
.It Fl -transmit-cert Ar path , Fl -transmit-key Ar path , Fl -transmit-ca Ar path
Client certificate, its private key, and the CA bundle to verify the server
against. Certificates and bearer tokens are both supported, and both may be
used at once: some estates will not distribute tokens and some cannot run an
internal CA.
.It Fl -transmit-insecure
Do not verify the server's certificate. Prints a warning on every run,
.Fl -quiet
included.
.It Fl -transmit-batch-lines Ar n , Fl -transmit-batch-bytes Ar size
Maximum records and maximum uncompressed bytes per request; default 2000 and
1MiB. Whichever limit trips first ends the batch.
.It Fl -transmit-attempts Ar n , Fl -transmit-timeout Ar duration
Attempts per request including the first (default 5, exponential backoff with
jitter), and the deadline for one request rather than for the whole upload
(default 60s). 5xx and network failures are retried; 4xx are not, except 429.
.It Fl -version
Print the
.Nm
version, the git commit it was built from, and the version of Syft compiled
into it; then exit 0.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width "0" -compact
.It 0
Success; a complete inventory was written.
.It 1
Partial success; output was written but at least one cataloger failed, and
.Ql scan.incomplete
is true in the report. An inventory missing one ecosystem is far more useful
than no inventory.
.It 2
Usage error: an unknown flag, an invalid exclusion pattern, or conflicting
options.
.It 3
Fatal: the scan source could not be constructed, or the output could not be
written.
.It 4
The
.Fl -timeout
deadline was exceeded.
.It 5
A source could not be enumerated: a package database is present on this host
and could not be read. The report is still written and
.Ql scan.sources
names the source and the reason, but the component count is wrong by an
unknown amount - and a small valid inventory looks exactly like a healthy
scan of a minimal machine, which is why this is not folded into 1.
.It 6
Transmission did not complete, or completed and the server's stored count
disagreed with the manifest. The report files are written first and the
spooled scan is kept, so this never costs the local copy.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "/proc/self/mountinfo" -compact
.It Pa /var/lib/swinv
Default output directory.
.It Pa /etc/machine-id
Primary source of the stable host identifier, with
.Pa /var/lib/dbus/machine-id
as a fallback.
.It Pa /etc/os-release
Operating-system identity, with
.Pa /usr/lib/os-release
as a fallback.
.It Pa /proc/self/mountinfo
Read to discover non-local filesystems to exclude.
.It Pa /proc/net/tcp
The four socket tables
.Pq Pa tcp , tcp6 , udp , udp6 ,
listing what is bound. World-readable, so the endpoints are always found.
.It Pa /proc/<pid>/fd
Attributes a listening socket to the process holding it. Readable only by the
process owner, so an unprivileged scan attributes only its own.
.It Pa /proc/<pid>/ns/net
Groups processes by network namespace, anchored on init's. This is what
separates a socket reachable at this machine's addresses from one reachable
only inside a container.
.It Pa /proc/<pid>/root
The filesystem of a containerised process, read to identify the software it
runs from that container's own package database.
.It Pa /etc/docker/daemon.json
Read only for
.Ql userland-proxy ,
whose absence would otherwise make a host publishing many ports look like one
publishing none.
.It Pa /sys/class/dmi/id
Hardware vendor, product name, and - for root only - serial and UUID.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Scan the running system and write JSON and CSV to the default directory:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
swinv
.Ed
.Pp
Keep one file per run forever, plus a stable
.Ql -latest
symlink:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
swinv --output-mode timestamped --latest-symlink
.Ed
.Pp
Operating-system packages only, straight to standard output, for a pipeline:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
swinv --catalogers os --format json --stdout | jq '.components | length'
.Ed
.Pp
Produce a CycloneDX document for a downstream vulnerability scanner:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
swinv --format cyclonedx-json --out /var/lib/swinv
grype sbom:/var/lib/swinv/$(hostname -s)-latest.cdx.json
.Ed
.Pp
List what is exposed at the network edge, with the software behind each port:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
sudo swinv --out /var/lib/swinv
jq -r '.exposure[] | select(.bind_scope != "loopback")
       | [.address, .port, (.components // ["?"] | join(","))] | @tsv' \e
   /var/lib/swinv/*-latest.json
.Ed
.Pp
.Nm
reads no firewall, no NAT table and no security group, so
.Ql bind_scope
describes the bind and never reachability. A port published by a netfilter
rule with no process behind it - Kubernetes
.Ql NodePort ,
Docker with
.Ql userland-proxy
disabled, rootful Podman's default netavark - has no listening socket and
is invisible here. Every report names what it could not see in
.Ql scan.exposure_blind_spots ,
and a short exposure list means nothing until that has been read.
.Pp
Ask what is listening and what installed it, which needs root:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
sudo swinv --out /var/lib/swinv
jq -r '.services[] | select(.confidence == "medium") | .executable' \
   /var/lib/swinv/*-latest.json
.Ed
.Pp
That last query lists the software that is serving traffic and that no package
manager installed, which is the finding a package inventory cannot produce.
.Pp
Scan a mounted disk image rather than the live system:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
swinv --root /mnt/image --out ./inventory --no-auto-exclude-mounts
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr systemd.timer 5 ,
.Xr systemd.exec 5
.Pp
.Nm
is normally driven by
.Pa swinv.timer ,
a daily systemd timer with a one-hour randomised delay.
.Sh STANDARDS
Output conforms to the swinv JSON schema recorded in
.Ql schema_version ,
and, for the CycloneDX format, to CycloneDX 1.6. The CSV is RFC 4180 quoted,
UTF-8, without a byte-order mark.
.Sh AUTHORS
.Nm
is Apache-2.0 licensed and builds on
.Lk https://github.com/anchore/syft Syft ,
also Apache-2.0.
.Sh CAVEATS
Two runs on an unchanged machine produce byte-identical output apart from the
timestamps in the
.Ql scan
object, which is what makes these files diffable. Anything that breaks that
property is a bug.
